  "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =15{x}^{4}+60{x}^{3}-2 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(x) & =60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{\u2033}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{3}+180{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 60{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{\u2033}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2033}(-4) & =60(-4)^{3}+180(-4)^{2}=-960<0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(-1) & =60(-1)^{3}+180(-1)^{2}=120>0 \\\\ {g}^{\u2033}(1) & =60(1)^{3}+180(1)^{2}=240>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is a point of inflection.",
